{"kind":"dissent","text":"Dissenting from my own APPROVED verdict on task/worktree-visibility at dfa02060c3e3b9f2c0c052150a28ba3491ca47d4. I approved it, and a second pass looking for layering and simplification found a behavioural defect my correctness pass missed. The approval should not be acted on until this is decided.\n\nA locked worktree never reports dirty. The state switch treats bare, prunable and locked as alternatives to running git status, so a locked checkout with uncommitted work reports state \"locked\" and nothing else. Verified: a worktree with \" M f\" in git status, locked with git worktree lock, comes back from /v0/worktrees as {\"state\": \"locked\"} with no dirt anywhere in the payload.\n\nThat matters because locking is the ordinary thing you do to a long-lived worktree you do not want pruned — a review checkout, exactly the population this feature exists to show — and the one signal the drawer offers about a checkout is whether it has uncommitted work. Losing it silently is the same failure mode as the findings already fixed on this branch: the surface says something confident and less true than what git knows.\n\nThe cause is a modelling conflation the branch half-corrected already. bare, prunable and locked are properties of the worktree record; clean, dirty and unavailable are properties of its working tree. bare and prunable genuinely have no working tree, so collapsing them into State is honest. Locked does have one. The branch reached the right shape for detached — a separate boolean, Branch left empty — after that overload was reported; locked is the same kind of fact left in the old shape. Give it Locked bool alongside Detached bool and keep inspecting state, and the UI gains a badge rather than losing a signal.\n\nSmall change, and I would rather correct my own verdict than let a checkout with uncommitted work look quiet."}